<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Almoravid Qubba</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">12th century</span> · Also known as Qubba Ba'adiyyin, Qubba Barudiyyin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Almoravid Qubba, also known as the Qubba Ba'adiyyin, is a small domed pavilion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, built in the early twelfth century under the Almoravid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-yusuf">Ali ibn Yusuf</a>, probably as an ablutions building for his great mosque. Uncovered in 1952, it is the only intact Almoravid monument in the city, its interior dome richly carved with interlacing arches, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muqarnas">muqarnas</a> and plant decoration.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/almoravid-qubba.jpg" alt="Almoravid Qubba" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: R Prazeres · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Almoravid_Qubba_IMG_5629.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
